Show the layer's loading screen while a game starts, in place of the plain status screen.

The status screen spoke to a developer: monospaced status lines ("Preparing original guest
executable…", "Keep Tolkara open."), the elapsed time and the file the game wrote last, on the
system background. Brighter Pocket's players see a plainer loading screen instead
(launcher/Pocket/PocketLoadingView.m, added by the layer): the game's name, the step in plain
words, and a progress bar that follows the runtime log's lines from a background queue while
the game holds the main thread. statusController makes it the screen's view; the status label,
the elapsed time, the startup activity and Diagnostics are the same objects as before, under
its Details, so every status line still lands where the launcher puts it. A start that ends
(showStartStopped, refuseNativeGame) tells the screen, which then shows the failure in full;
runNativeGame hands it the log as it opens it. Upstream candidate in a general form: a hook
for a per-app startup screen around Tolkara's status views; as it stands it names this layer's
view.

